Speaker 1 pitched an AI automation tool for small businesses; the team raised friction and targeting concerns and agreed to spend 15 minutes researching before regrouping.



Initial Pitch

Small businesses know AI can help but are too busy or don't know how to use it

Idea came from a business owner making thousands by automating everything

Flow: short survey (no more than 15 questions) finds time-consuming areas, then Claude generates an automation

Showing estimated time and money saved is key to selling the idea

Alternative: a library of ready automations for common tools like CRMs, delivery routing, spreadsheets



Concerns Raised

Speaker 3 felt the survey adds too much friction and 15 questions may not capture accurate data

Speaker 2 and Speaker 3 felt the idea is too broad and needs a narrower niche

Trust is a risk: savings claims may feel like a scam, like car insurance ads on TikTok

Speaker 2 suggested a voice-based option, since many shop owners aren't tech-savvy



Target Market

Speaker 1 wants to target in-person, brick-and-mortar businesses, since they need it most

Examples: dog groomers, bakeries; orders, sales tracking, shift planning, delivery routes, stock

Marketing and inventory floated as focus areas; AI marketing risks producing low-quality 'slop'

Solutions need not be strict automation; other AI proposals are fine



Research Plan

Use data from 50 anonymous small businesses plus outside research on biggest time sinks and repetitive tasks

Goal is to converge on one idea the whole team agrees on



Next Steps

(Everyone) Research and review the 50-business data for about 15 minutes, then regroup to compare findings







